.TH tkutlstate n 0.2 tkutils
.SH tkutils::tkutlstate
.PP
Save and restore a \fBtablelist\fR's column layout — widths, hidden state, display order and the active sort — so an application can persist how the user arranged a table between sessions. Library\-neutral.
.SS Package
.CS
package require tkutils::tkutlstate 0.1
.CE
.PP
Requires \fBTk\fR and \fBtablelist\fR.
.SS Commands
.CS
::tkutils::tkutlstate::save            tbl          ;# -> state dict
::tkutils::tkutlstate::restore         tbl state
::tkutils::tkutlstate::saveToFile      tbl path
::tkutils::tkutlstate::restoreFromFile tbl path      ;# -> 1 applied, 0 missing
::tkutils::tkutlstate::saveVia         tbl key setPrefix   ;# {*}$setPrefix key dict
::tkutils::tkutlstate::restoreVia      tbl key getPrefix   ;# -> 1 applied, 0 missing
::tkutils::tkutlstate::autosave        tbl key setPrefix ?-delay ms?
::tkutils::tkutlstate::cancelAutosave  tbl
.CE
.PP
\fBtbl\fR is a raw tablelist. For a \fBtkutils::tkutablelist\fR wrapper, pass \fB[tkutils::tkutablelist::tableWidget $w]\fR.
.PP
\fBsaveVia\fR/\fBrestoreVia\fR are \fBstorage\-neutral\fR: you inject a setter/getter command \fIprefix\fR and the module appends the key (and, for the setter, the state dict). They work against a file, an array, or a database settings table without the module knowing the backend. \fBautosave\fR persists automatically \-\- debounced \-\- whenever the user resizes or reorders a column.
.PP
The state is a plain Tcl dict, e.g.
.CS
version 1 ncols 4 columns {{width 20 hide 0} ...} columnorder {...} \
    sortcolumn 1 sortorder decreasing
.CE
.PP
so it is safe to store as text, embed in a config, or convert to JSON.
.SS Usage
.CS
package require Tk
package require tablelist
package require tkutils::tkutlstate

# on quit
::tkutils::tkutlstate::saveToFile .t [file join $cfgdir table.state]

# on start (after the table is built)
::tkutils::tkutlstate::restoreFromFile .t [file join $cfgdir table.state]
.CE
.SS "Persist to a database settings table (autosave)"
.PP
Given a settings store with \fBsettingSet db key value\fR / \fBsettingGet db key\fR:
.CS
set raw [::tkutils::tkutablelist::tableWidget $w]

# on open, after the table is built and filled:
::tkutils::tkutlstate::restoreVia $raw overview \
    [list ::app::store::settingGet $db]
::tkutils::tkutlstate::autosave   $raw overview \
    [list ::app::store::settingSet $db]
.CE
.PP
Column widths and order are then written back (debounced) as the user adjusts them, and reapplied next time the table opens. \fBcancelAutosave $raw\fR stops it.
.SS In\-memory
.CS
set st [::tkutils::tkutlstate::save .t]
# ... later ...
::tkutils::tkutlstate::restore .t $st
.CE
.SS Notes
.IP \(bu
\fBrestore\fR only touches columns that exist in both the saved state and the current table, so it degrades gracefully when the table definition changed.
.IP \(bu
\fBcolumnorder\fR is captured/applied when the tablelist build supports it (wrapped in \fBcatch\fR).
.IP \(bu
Save the state \fIafter\fR the user is done arranging (e.g. on window close), not on every column resize.
.SS "Error codes"
.PP
\fB\-errorcode {TKUTILS TKUTLSTATE STATE}\fR when given a non\-state value.
